WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.200
There are paintings that contain&nbsp;
more details than the human eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.960
can process. And this might be&nbsp;
the most extreme of them all.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.280
The work you are looking at right now was&nbsp;
painted almost six hundred years ago. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.960
although it measures barely sixty-six centimeters,&nbsp;
within it there is an entire city with hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.440
buildings. A bridge full of tiny people. A garden&nbsp;
where every flower can be identified species by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.880
species. And complete biblical scenes,&nbsp;
carved into capitals the size of a coin.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.520
You may not be able to grasp just how&nbsp;
incredible this painting is right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.200
But after watching this video, you&nbsp;
might also find yourself wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.320
how a human being could have painted&nbsp;
this almost six hundred years ago.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.480
This work is called The Madonna of&nbsp;
Chancellor Rolin. It was painted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:53.200
Jan van Eyck around 1435, and today it&nbsp;
is held at the Louvre Museum in Paris.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.760
If you are a follower of this channel, you&nbsp;
will remember Van Eyck for having painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.280
other works such as the Arnolfini Portrait, or&nbsp;
The Virgin and Child with Canon van der Paele,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.040
works we have already analyzed on this channel.&nbsp;
And once again, the Flemish master delivers an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.760
image that seems impossible to have been&nbsp;
painted by hand in the fifteenth century.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.960
Unlike his other paintings, in this scene&nbsp;
we see an elegant palace opening onto an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.400
infinite landscape. On the left there is a man&nbsp;
kneeling with his hands in prayer and an open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.320
book in front of him. And on the right, we see the&nbsp;
Virgin Mary holding the Christ Child on her lap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.520
while an angel with multicolored wings&nbsp;
descends to crown her as Queen of Heaven.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.400
The Child raises his right hand to bless the&nbsp;
praying man. And in his left hand he holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.400
a small crystal orb topped with a cross:&nbsp;
the symbol of his dominion over the world.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.160
Without a doubt, this work is a sacred scene,&nbsp;
painted with supernatural detail. But the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.160
thing we must ask ourselves is: who is this&nbsp;
man appearing face to face with the Virgin?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.600
This man is Nicolas Rolin. And his story is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.080
the most extraordinary rises&nbsp;
of the entire Middle Ages.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.000
Rolin was not someone born into nobility.&nbsp;
In fact, this man came from a common family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.800
without titles or lineage. But thanks&nbsp;
to his intelligence and his ambition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.720
he managed to climb his way up&nbsp;
to become Chancellor of Burgundy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.400
the right hand of Duke Philip the Good, and in&nbsp;
turn, one of the richest men in all of Europe.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.360
His contemporaries did not always speak well&nbsp;
of him. In the books he is described as a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:37.680
of voracious greed, obsessed with accumulating&nbsp;
wealth and power. The definition of a cunning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.720
politician who controlled the strings of&nbsp;
one of the most powerful states of his time.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.160
And when he commissioned this&nbsp;
painting for his family's chapel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.720
Rolin made a request that broke&nbsp;
all the rules of religious art.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.040
In the medieval tradition, when a&nbsp;
donor appeared in a sacred painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.400
they were usually depicted&nbsp;
small, kneeling in a corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.600
and always presented by an intercessor saint&nbsp;
who would accompany them before the divine.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.760
However, as we see in this painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.840
Rolin eliminated the intercessor. And on top&nbsp;
of that, he had himself painted exactly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.000
same size as the Virgin Mary. Face to&nbsp;
face with her. As if they were equals.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.400
So a common man, even as chancellor,&nbsp;
presenting himself before the Mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.920
of God at her same level, in the fifteenth&nbsp;
century, was a display of pure arrogance.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.400
Now let us look at the composition carefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.560
because Van Eyck built this painting&nbsp;
as a universe divided in two.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.680
If we follow the line of the floor tiles,&nbsp;
we can see that an invisible division runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.560
through the entire scene. On the left we&nbsp;
find the world of Rolin, and on the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.640
the world of Mary and the Child. And that division&nbsp;
continues into the landscape in the background.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.600
On Rolin's side stretches an earthly&nbsp;
city, full of houses, businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.160
and worldly life. While on the Virgin's&nbsp;
side, the view is dominated by church&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:02.400
towers rising toward the sky. Some historians&nbsp;
see here the philosophy of Saint Augustine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.000
representing the city of men and the city&nbsp;
of God, coexisting in a single image.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.400
Right at the center of this division we&nbsp;
can see a river. Which could be seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.520
the border separating the earthly from the divine.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.200
But curiously, Van Eyck also painted a bridge,&nbsp;
which following this line of thought, could also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.480
symbolize the union between both worlds. And&nbsp;
if we count its arches, we discover there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.400
exactly seven. The sacred number par excellence.&nbsp;
So the bridge connecting the world of men with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.960
the world of God does not have just any structure.&nbsp;
It is built upon the number of divine perfection.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.640
And now we come to what makes&nbsp;
this painting truly unique.

