00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.320
Now then, let us continue.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.600
To begin talking about the impressive details of&nbsp;
this painting, we must start with the garden seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.440
behind the figures. It is an enclosed garden,&nbsp;
a traditional symbol of Mary's virginity. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:27.040
Van Eyck did not paint generic flowers&nbsp;
in it. He painted identifiable species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.320
each with its own meaning. Among them we&nbsp;
can see white lilies representing purity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.920
Roses evoking the suffering of Christ,&nbsp;
and irises symbolizing the pain of Mary.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.320
Next to the garden we also see two&nbsp;
peacocks. In the Christian tradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.440
these animals were symbols of immortality and at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.400
the same time of pride. A sin that&nbsp;
a man like Rolin knew quite well.&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.600
But the most incredible details of the entire&nbsp;
painting are above the chancellor's head.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.560
If we look at the capitals of the columns,&nbsp;
we discover that Van Eyck carved complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.720
biblical scenes into paint. The expulsion of&nbsp;
Adam and Eve from paradise. Cain murdering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.680
his brother Abel, and the drunkenness&nbsp;
of Noah with the ark floating above him.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.320
Each of these scenes measures approximately two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.240
and a half centimeters, which could&nbsp;
be compared to the size of a coin.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:23.120
And within those two and a half centimeters,&nbsp;
there is something even more astonishing.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:28.080
In the scene of Noah, Van Eyck painted&nbsp;
four men next to the patriarch. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.040
according to the Bible, Noah only had&nbsp;
three sons. At this point in the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.800
you too are probably doubting that&nbsp;
someone like Van Eyck could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.800
made a careless mistake like this, being so&nbsp;
meticulous. And indeed, it was not a mistake.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.240
Nicolas Rolin had four sons. So Van Eyck&nbsp;
deliberately altered the biblical story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:53.280
to draw a parallel with his client's life.&nbsp;
Perhaps a personal message that most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.560
paid no attention to for a long time,&nbsp;
since it took centuries to be discovered.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.880
But there is still one final secret.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.200
If we look beyond the garden, next to the&nbsp;
battlements overlooking the landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:10.800
there are two small figures with their backs&nbsp;
turned, contemplating the river and the city.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.120
Honestly, no matter how much I have&nbsp;
searched for information about this detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.840
nobody knows for certain who they are.&nbsp;
But there is a fascinating theory.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.440
One of the figures wears a red turban. Exactly&nbsp;
the same type of red turban that appears in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:31.280
famous portrait at the National Gallery in London,&nbsp;
which is believed to be Van Eyck's self-portrait.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.880
If we associate this small detail with the artist,&nbsp;
in this painting Jan van Eyck would have used the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:42.240
same strategy we have already seen in others&nbsp;
of his works. Like in the Arnolfini Portrait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.880
where he painted himself reflected in the&nbsp;
mirror at the back, or in The Virgin and Child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.000
with Canon van der Paele, where he presumably&nbsp;
appears reflected in Saint George's shield.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.000
So if we assume that this small&nbsp;
man is Jan van Eyck, once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.280
the master would have hidden himself inside his&nbsp;
own work. But this time with his back turned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:09.280
contemplating an imaginary&nbsp;
world that he himself painted.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:17.440
Thank you for watching the video until the&nbsp;
end, and I will see you in the next video.

